# Normalise line endings so the committed bytes are the same on every
# machine. Without this, a file written on Windows lands as CRLF and the
# same file written on Linux lands as LF: the content is identical and the
# bytes differ, which shows up as "the whole file changed" after a reader
# regenerates it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The PUBLISHED ARTEFACTS are not line-ending normalised.
#
# A reader downloads a committed sweep, regenerates it, and compares the
# bytes. If git normalises the line endings on the way in, the file in
# the repository and the file the reader regenerates differ by a single
# byte pattern -- and `git status` cannot see the difference, because
# normalisation is exactly what git does before it decides.
#
# These files are written with explicit LF (`newline="\n"`) so the
# bytes are the same either way. Marking them as -text makes that a
# property of the repository rather than of the machine that happened to
# write them, and it means a CRLF version is a real diff instead of an
# invisible one.
hedging_agent/fixtures/*.csv -text
outputs/*.json -text
cards/*.md -text

# Binary files that must never be line-ending translated.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.parquet binary
*.xlsx binary
*.docx binary
*.pptx binary
